exports[`the Introduction: write, reject with a point, fix, accept the medium brief lists the ready steps with the lead token filled in: section-lead brief 1`] = `
"work brief a1 · section-lead (medium) · t1 The Introduction
snapshot: tsd-writing@1
dir: <project> (run every work command in this folder)

YOU ARE  medium tier. You run one task. You give its units to low-tier roles, see that each rule gets evidence
         from its checker, and decide each result of your makers: accept it, or reject it. You do
         not change directions; you escalate when they do not fit.
ROLE     Runs one section - assigns the writer and the linter, and decides the section.
         - Assign the writer, then the linter on each result, with work assign.
         - Escalate with work escalate when the directions do not fit.
TASK     Write the Introduction from the agreed facts.
WORK IN  docs/tsd.md (source tsd); verify: work-lint docs/tsd.md (run it in the dir; exit 0 = ready)
READ     docs/facts.md#dedupe
RULES    no-code-proof  No file paths with line numbers, no commit hashes in the body  (linter)
         no-ticket-ids  No ticket ids and no chat-thread citations  (linter)
         repeatable     A reviewer can repeat the problem without the design  (section-lead)

READY
  make t1 The Introduction
    work assign a1.<token> t1 --role=writer

YOUR TURN
  assign      work assign a1.<token> <task> --role=<writer|linter> [--max-attempts=<n>]
              Do a unit yourself only when no lower role exists for it. Give the subagent exactly the printed command. One maker writes a file at a time; when the assign says that a maker waits for a file, give its command only when work next says that its turn has come.
  show        work assignment show <assignment>
              Read a child's brief before you give its command; it takes no snapshot.
  result      work result show <result>
              Shows each review of a result, with its evidence and points. Read it before you decide.
  accept      work review a1.<token> <result> accept --evidence="repeatable | <where you looked> | <what you saw>"
              Read the latest result itself, not a diff. One --evidence line for each rule that you check, or, with no rule, the place in the result and what you read there; what you saw in a sentence of words.
  reject      work review a1.<token> <result> reject --point="<what is wrong, and where>" --breaks=<rule id>
              One --point for each problem, with the rule that it breaks; a Reject uses an attempt of the maker (a fault). A point that no rule covers needs a direction change: first escalate it with work escalate a1.<token> --result=<result> --reason="<the point>". When your parent resolves it, accept or reject the result; a point with no rule in your Reject then waits for a draft that changes the direction.
  recheck     work recheck a1.<token> <result> --reason="<what changed outside, or what a page review found>"
              Asks the maker for a new result. Use it when work next says a result is made from an old revision of a source, or to reopen an accepted part that must change.
  dreview     work review a1.<token> <draft> accept --evidence="<where you read in the draft> | <what you saw>", or reject --reason="<why>"
              Read it first with work direction show <draft>, and ask each question of work guide direction-review. Never review a draft that you or an assignment above you wrote.
  ask         work ask a1.<token> --text="<the context first, then the question>" [--to=owner]
              Ask your parent. Ask the person (--to=owner) only for a fact that no direction, rule, or guide gives, and that stops the work; never about a limit or a choice that a rule settles. The top agent forwards the person's answer, and the person confirms it.
  answer      work answer a1.<token> <question> --text="<the answer>"
              Answer only questions that name you; work waiting --for=a1 lists them.
  resolve     work resolve a1.<token> <escalation> --resolution="<the agreement, or the direction, in words>"
              Resolve an escalation of a result by your children (work waiting --for=a1 lists them); an escalation with no result is a question, and you answer it. A resolve accepts and rejects nothing; the deciders of the result then accept or reject it. When the answer needs a direction that the directions do not give, escalate to your parent instead.
  escalate    work escalate a1.<token> --reason="<what is missing, and what you tried>" [--result=<result>]
              Escalate when the directions do not fit, when you cannot decide, or when work passes a limit whose action you cannot do (work guide limits); put the words of the refusal in the reason. When a maker used its last try on a fault, the task waits for the evaluation of the high role; give no new maker before work next says so.
  free        work free a1.<token> <child assignment>
              Only when the child's subagent stopped in the middle; the next work brief claims it again.
  finish      work finish a1.<token> [<child assignment>]
              Finish each child when the task is accepted, then yourself. Finish a maker when its part is accepted, or when it has no attempts left, and another maker waits for its file. work next prints these steps.
  forward     work forward a1.<token> <person> <reject|ask|comment|answer|resolve> <id> --quote="<their exact words>" [--point="<one problem>" --breaks=<rule id>] --channel=chat
              Only the person's own words; split a reject into points with the rule each breaks. A reject, ask, or comment counts at once; an answer or resolve waits for their work confirm. A resolve names the escalation as <id>, and the person's words are its resolution. The person does not accept results: a lead decides them, and the person rejects a result only after its deciders accept it.
  source      work source a1.<token> <source>@<revision>
              Run it after you read a source outside git (for example a version of a page), with the revision that you read. The record holds for outsideSourceHours (72 by default); then work next asks you to read the source again. A git source needs no record.
  checkpoint  work checkpoint a1.<token> t1 --next="<the first thing to do>"
              Save before a pause; work resume a1.<token> t1 shows what changed.
When the task is accepted, run each step in READY (put the work on its base, finish each child, then yourself); then STOP. When each step in READY only waits, STOP: the page wakes you when a step changes, so do not run work next again to check. STOP also when you escalate. A question only in chat is not in the record; escalate it. When work passes a limit and you cannot do its action (work guide limits), escalate it with the words of the refusal; do not ask the person.

(read-only: no snapshot, no claim)"
`;

